From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Casota <dcasota@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] installer: ISO installs follow the boot firmware and release
 DHCP leases

Two defects of an install from the ISO, fixed together because the
second depends on the first.

1. Bootmode and live. Since 1e80f1802 _add_defaults() sets bootmode to
   'efi' when the configuration names none, the curses UI never names
   one, and nothing reads the firmware that booted the media: an
   interactive install on a legacy-BIOS VM leaves a GPT with only the ESP
   and the root partition, no boot code in sector 0 and no
   /boot/grub2/i386-pc, so the firmware finds no bootable disk. The same
   install runs with live=False, because isoInstaller stamps live only on
   a non-empty kickstart configuration: the CD is not ejected, efibootmgr
   is not run and /etc/machine-id stays uninitialized. isoInstaller now
   hands its media defaults to Installer.configure(), which applies them
   with setdefault() once the configuration exists, from a kickstart or
   from the UI: live=True, and bootmode 'efi' when /sys/firmware/efi
   exists or the machine is aarch64, 'dualboot' otherwise. Values a
   kickstart names win; image builds call configure() without defaults
   and are unchanged.

2. DHCP leases. The installer environment holds DHCPv4 leases that it
   never releases (its .network sets SendRelease=no); the installed system
   then asks the same server with a new client identity and VMware's
   vmnetdhcp, which binds leases to the MAC, does not answer it, so the
   installed system boots without an address. Before the manifest is
   written, a live install now stops the DHCP client of every link that
   holds a DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 address: dhcprelease.release_leases() writes a
   runtime override per link (DHCP=no, SendRelease=yes) under
   /run/systemd/network, reloads networkd once and waits until every link
   reports the override applied and no DHCP address left.

   Link state comes from `networkctl --json=short list` (Name, NetworkFile,
   Addresses[].ConfigSource), the public description networkd gives in
   systemd 253 and 257 alike, not from the private files under
   /run/systemd/netif. All links share one reload and one deadline, so
   the worst case does not grow with the number of NICs: at most three
   setup commands of COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SEC (10 s) and RELEASE_TIMEOUT_SEC
   (10 s) of polling every POLL_INTERVAL_SEC (0.2 s), 40 s in all, every
   poll capped by the time left. It runs only when networkd is active (so
   it never starts it), skips link names that are globs, and never
   raises: a failure must not fail an otherwise good install. The outcome
   per link (released, timeout, skipped) with any error and the duration
   goes to the installer log and to dhcp_release in the manifest, of
   which the installer writes a copy to /var/log/poi on the installed
   system, the one record that survives the reboot. It is gated on
   live, the flag the installer already uses for changes to its own
   environment (networkd restart, CD eject), and part 1 is what makes
   live true for interactive installs.

tests/test_dhcprelease.py covers the release logic with networkctl and
systemctl faked: networkd inactive, no DHCP links, release after the
override applies, one reload and one deadline for several links, a
vanished link, glob names, a failing command, unexpected JSON, and a
timeout on every command. Run with: pytest -x tests/test_dhcprelease.py

The release drives networkd through networkctl, and the installer's
initrd builder deleted /usr/bin/networkctl to save space, so on a real ISO
the release failed with FileNotFoundError before it did anything. Keep
networkctl in the initrd. Should it be missing all the same, the release
records "networkctl is not installed" instead of an unexplained OSError.
A unit test holds networkctl out of the initrd removal list.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
